SOS Wallet — How It Works (Built on the SOS69069 Protocol)

The SOS Wallet is not a traditional cryptocurrency wallet. It is a mint-based ledger that uses the on-chain SOS69069 contract as its protocol and only source of truth.

Every balance, every transfer-like interaction, and every recovery operation is expressed exclusively through mint events ("pushTo"). Nothing can be modified, censored, reversed, or seized because the protocol stores only a permanent, append-only history of these mint events.

---

Core Protocol Mechanics

The SOS69069 contract defines only a single value-bearing operation:

- "pushTo(address to)"
- "pushForMe()" (a convenience function)

Each successful call permanently mints exactly 1 SOS.

The protocol maintains three primary counters:

- trustOf[address] — Total number of times the address has been the recipient of a push. This is the value displayed by "balanceOf()".
- pushOf[address] — Total number of pushes initiated by the address.
- phase69069 (totalSupply) — Global protocol counter representing total minted supply.

Unlike traditional ERC-20 tokens, SOS69069 contains:

- no "transfer()"
- no "approve()"
- no allowances
- no burn function
- no DEX integration
- no ownership
- no custody mechanism

Tokens never move after being minted. Value is created only through new mint events and accumulates permanently.

All protocol counters ("trustOf", "pushOf", and "phase69069") operate as cyclic "uint256" values. When reaching 2²⁵⁶ − 1, they wrap back to zero while permanent overflow counters preserve the complete lifetime history.

---

Balance Definition

The effective protocol balance is defined as:

Balance = trustOf − pushOf

Where:

- trustOf represents all SOS ever received.
- pushOf represents all SOS ever pushed to others.

A positive balance indicates an address has received more trust than it has distributed.

A zero or negative effective position indicates the address has pushed as much as, or more than, it has received.

---

The Trust Gate

SOS69069 replaces token transfers with forward minting.

Minting is therefore the transfer mechanism, but it is governed by trust.

A wallet may only mint forward when:

trustOf > pushOf

Receiving new mints increases "trustOf", expanding the wallet's future minting capacity.

Every received mint therefore becomes newly available trust that can later be forwarded to another participant.

This creates a natural reputation and credit system:

1. Another participant mints SOS to you.
2. Your "trustOf" increases.
3. Once "trustOf > pushOf", you gain the ability to mint to someone else.
4. Your push increases the recipient's "trustOf".
5. The trust chain continues indefinitely.

Self-pushes ("pushForMe()" or "pushTo(yourself)") are allowed and increase both "trustOf" and "pushOf".

---

Offers (Peer-to-Peer Exchange)

Because SOS69069 contains no transfer function, buying and selling occur through Offers.

Typical offers include:

- "I will mint X SOS to you if you pay Y."
- "I will pay Y if you mint X SOS to me."

Participants settle these agreements by executing the required "pushTo()" transactions.

The irreversible mint events themselves become the settlement record.

No centralized custody, automated market maker, liquidity pool, or on-chain order book is required.

---

Wallet Recovery

Recovery is performed entirely through verifiable mint activity.

The recovery procedure is a social and protocol convention layered on top of the immutable blockchain history.

Recovery process:

1. Create a new wallet.
2. From the new wallet, perform the first push to the original wallet.
3. This first push permanently binds the two addresses.
4. Continue pushing from the new wallet until its "pushOf" equals twice the old wallet's total push count.
5. Once the 2× threshold is reached, the new wallet inherits the complete history and identity of the previous wallet.

Recovery requires:

- no recovery company
- no seed phrase service
- no multisignature administrator
- no recovery deadline

Only publicly verifiable on-chain mint activity determines recovery.

---

Why SOS Cannot Be Censored, Modified, or Seized

All wallet state is reconstructed exclusively from:

- permanent "Pushed" / "Transfer" events
- "trustOf"
- "pushOf"

The contract:

- accepts no ETH
- has no owner
- contains no administrator
- has no pause function
- has no blacklist
- has no upgrade mechanism

Since existing balances never move and new value appears only through irreversible mint events, the protocol contains no mechanism for third parties to confiscate, reverse, or rewrite existing balances.

---

Wallet Interface

An SOS Wallet displays only protocol data:

- Current "trustOf"
- Current "pushOf"
- Effective balance ("trustOf − pushOf")
- Complete history of every mint sent and received
- Active peer-to-peer offers
- Recovery progress when binding a replacement wallet

Everything else—including:

- Name: 69069
- Symbol: SOS
- Decimals: 0
- ERC-20-compatible interface

exists solely to allow conventional wallets, block explorers, and blockchain tools to recognize and display the protocol.

The protocol itself is entirely defined by forward minting.

